@page "/terms"
@using Microsoft.Extensions.Options
@inject IOptions<SiteOptions> Site

<PageTitle>Terms · C# Advent</PageTitle>
<SocialMeta Title="Terms · C# Advent" Description="How C# Advent works, and what taking part means." />

<article class="max-w-3xl text-slate-700">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-900">Terms</h1>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-500">Last updated October 26, 2026</p>

    <p class="mt-6 text-lg">
        C# Advent is a community event: every December, people from the C# community each publish a post on their day. By
        claiming a spot, you agree to these terms.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-slate-900">Who runs C# Advent</h2>
    <p class="mt-3">
        Matt Groves started C# Advent in 2017, inspired by the F# Advent Calendar. Today it's organized by Matt Groves and
        Calvin Allen, as volunteers in their own time. It's an independent community project, not affiliated with Microsoft.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-slate-900">Taking part</h2>
    <ol class="mt-3 list-decimal space-y-3 pl-6">
        <li>
            <strong class="text-slate-900">Request a spot.</strong> You don't need a topic yet. The organizers review every
            request, and a spot is yours once it's approved.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong class="text-slate-900">Prepare your content</strong>, in English, and keep it to yourself until your day.
            Host it anywhere: your own site, dev.to, Medium, YouTube, GitHub Pages, or somewhere else.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong class="text-slate-900">Make it about C#</strong> in some way. Beyond that, it's up to you. It has to be your
            own work.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong class="text-slate-900">Open with the C# Advent blurb.</strong> Start your content by saying it's part of that
            year's C# Advent calendar, with a link to <a href="https://csadvent.christmas" class="text-advent-700 underline">csadvent.christmas</a>,
            so your readers can find the rest of the Advent. The wording is up to you; there's one ready to paste on your My spots
            page. We check every post for it automatically, and if it isn't there when your post is announced, a backup poster can
            take your spot.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong class="text-slate-900">Publish on your day</strong>, and share it with the hashtags #csharp and #csadvent.
            Each day is revealed on the calendar at midnight Eastern time.
        </li>
    </ol>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-slate-900">If you can't make it</h2>
    <p class="mt-3">
        Things happen. If you can't deliver your post, let us know as soon as you can, so someone on the backup list can take the
        spot. If your post isn't ready in time, or doesn't open with the C# Advent blurb when it's announced, we may give your
        spot to someone else. If nobody fills a spot, it shows "No post" on the calendar and in the archive, without your name.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-slate-900">What we can decline</h2>
    <p class="mt-3">
        We can turn down a request, or remove an entry, if it isn't about C#, isn't the author's own work, is spam or a sales
        pitch, or is hateful, harassing or otherwise inappropriate for a friendly community event.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-slate-900">Your content</h2>
    <p class="mt-3">
        Your post stays yours. We only show your name, your link, and your post's title and address, on the calendar and
        permanently in the archive, and we share them on C# Advent's social media accounts and feed, tagging the social accounts
        you gave us. Ask us if you'd like your entry taken down. Once the season is over, we check now and then that your post is
        still at its address. If it's gone, we send readers to a copy the Internet Archive's Wayback Machine saved of it, if there
        is one, until you tell us where it's moved.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-slate-900">Your information</h2>
    <p class="mt-3">
        Our <a href="privacy" class="text-advent-700 underline">privacy page</a> explains what we collect, how we use it, and
        when we delete it. Emails about your spot are part of taking part, so they always come; every other email we send has
        a link to unsubscribe. If you've had a spot, we keep your email address after the season so we can tell you when the
        next one opens, and we'll delete it whenever you ask.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-slate-900">No guarantees</h2>
    <p class="mt-3">
        We do our best to keep the site running and to share every post, but the site is run by volunteers and provided as it
        is, without any warranty. We aren't responsible for the content of the posts we link to; each author is.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-slate-900">Changes and questions</h2>
    <p class="mt-3">
        We may update these terms, and the date at the top shows when they last changed. Questions? Email
        <a href="mailto:@Site.Value.ContactEmail" class="text-advent-700 underline">@Site.Value.ContactEmail</a>.
    </p>
</article>
